   "source": [
    "# Notebook 05 — Ensemble Detector & Comprehensive Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Quantum Drift Detector · Phase 4**\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## What this notebook does\n",
    "\n",
    "In Notebooks 03 and 04 we saw individual detectors (CUSUM, BOCD, Window) applied to specific scenarios. Now we ask the harder questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Under what conditions does each method work best?**  \n",
    "> **How does temporal correlation in the noise affect detection performance?**\n",
    "\n",
    "These two questions map directly onto our two target PhD research groups:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Giarmatzi / Tonekaboni (correlated noise):** Their research focuses on *non-Markovian* noise — noise whose strength at time $t$ depends on its strength at time $t-1$. We will show that standard detectors struggle when $\\phi$ (the autocorrelation coefficient) is high, and that the ensemble is more robust.\n",
    "- **Sanders / Usman at CSIRO (quantum verification):** They need to know when a quantum device drifted during an experiment. We will show that the ensemble gives the best recall vs. false-alarm trade-off — the most reliable \"guard rail\" for an ongoing experiment.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Evaluation methodology  \n",
    "2. Part A — Noise-type comparison (depolarizing vs dephasing vs correlated)  \n",
    "3. Part B — Effect of temporal correlation (sweeping $\\phi$)  \n",
    "4. Part C — Effect of shift magnitude  \n",
    "5. Part D — Ensemble vs individual detectors  \n",
    "6. Key findings & connection to PhD research"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.ticker as mticker\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Package imports\n",
    "from quantum_drift_detector.simulators import (\n",
    "    DepolarizingSimulator, DephasingSimulator, CorrelatedNoiseSimulator\n",
    ")\n",
    "from quantum_drift_detector.detectors import (\n",
    "    run_cusum, run_bocd, run_window_detector, run_ensemble\n",
    ")\n",
    "from quantum_drift_detector.features import extract_bit_flip_rate\n",
    "from quantum_drift_detector.utils import (\n",
    "    run_benchmark, summarise_benchmark, evaluate_single_trial\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plotting defaults\n",
    "plt.rcParams.update({\n",
    "    'figure.dpi': 120,\n",
    "    'axes.spines.top': False,\n",
    "    'axes.spines.right': False,\n",
    "    'font.size': 11,\n",
    "})\n",
    "COLOURS = {\n",
    "    'CUSUM':    '#E07B39',\n",
    "    'BOCD':     '#5B8DB8',\n",
    "    'Window':   '#6BAB6D',\n",
    "    'Ensemble': '#9B59B6',\n",
    "}\n",
    "print('Imports OK.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 1. Evaluation methodology\n",
    "\n",
    "### How do we score a detector?\n",
    "\n",
    "Each detector is run on a synthetic time series of length 200 timesteps. The true changepoint is always at $t = 100$ (the midpoint). We use 500 shots per timestep.\n",
    "\n",
    "We run **30 independent trials** per scenario (each trial uses a different random seed), and collect three metrics:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Definition | What it measures |\n",
    "|--------|-----------|------------------|\n",
    "| **Recall** | Fraction of trials where detector fires within 15 steps after $t=100$ | Was the change found? |\n",
    "| **False-alarm rate** | Fraction of trials where detector fires *before* $t=100$ | Did it cry wolf? |\n",
    "| **Mean delay** | Average timesteps between $t=100$ and alarm (over true-positive trials) | How fast was detection? |\n",
    "\n",
    "We use fixed, \"generic\" detector parameters that do not require knowledge of the true shift size. This is the realistic setting where an analyst monitors a device without knowing what kind of drift to expect."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# ── Detector runners ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "# Each runner accepts a 1D bit_flip_rates array and returns a dict with\n",
    "# at least a 'detected_at' key (int or None).\n",
    "#\n",
    "# Parameters are set to \"good generic defaults\":\n",
    "#   - CUSUM: target 2% baseline, catch shifts > ~1 pp, medium threshold\n",
    "#   - BOCD:  2% prior mean, shot-noise obs_var for 500 shots at 2%\n",
    "#   - Window: 10-step windows, moderate KL threshold\n",
    "#   - Ensemble: auto-baseline, soft voting\n",
    "\n",
    "DETECTOR_RUNNERS = {\n",
    "    'CUSUM':  lambda r: run_cusum(r,\n",
    "                  target_mean=np.mean(r[:20]),   # estimated from first 10%\n",
    "                  allowance=0.01,\n",
    "                  threshold=0.10),\n",
    "    'BOCD':   lambda r: run_bocd(r,\n",
    "                  mu_0=np.mean(r[:20]),\n",
    "                  obs_var=2.5e-5,\n",
    "                  prior_var=0.01,\n",
    "                  threshold=0.30),\n",
    "    'Window': lambda r: run_window_detector(r,\n",
    "                  window_size=10,\n",
    "                  threshold=0.05),\n",
    "    'Ensemble': lambda r: run_ensemble(r,\n",
    "                  method='soft',\n",
    "                  threshold=0.50),\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "DETECTOR_ORDER = ['CUSUM', 'BOCD', 'Window', 'Ensemble']\n",
    "print('Detector runners defined.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Part A — Noise-type comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "Three noise models, three shift magnitudes each. This gives a 9-scenario grid.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Noise models:**\n",
    "- **Depolarizing** — the simplest model; bit-flip probability jumps from $p_0$ to $p_1$. Independent shot-to-shot.\n",
    "- **Dephasing** — a Ramsey-experiment model (relevant to neutral-atom platforms). Bit-flip rate is $\\frac{1 - e^{-\\gamma}}{2}$, bounded in $[0, 0.5]$. The signal is \"compressed\" relative to depolarizing.\n",
    "- **Correlated (AR(1))** — the non-Markovian model. Noise strength at time $t$ depends on $t-1$ with coefficient $\\phi = 0.7$. The mean shifts, but the signal fluctuates more slowly, making the edge harder to pin down."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
