    "### Interpretation — Part C\n",
    "\n",
    "**The detection threshold:**  There is a characteristic shift size below which recall drops steeply.  This threshold depends on the noise level and the number of shots.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The fundamental limitation here is *shot noise*: with 500 shots at a 1% error rate, the standard deviation of the estimated rate per timestep is $\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N} \\approx 0.004$.  A shift of $\\Delta\\mu = 0.005$ is barely larger than one standard deviation — essentially invisible in a single timestep.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **CUSUM** is fastest to recover recall as the shift grows, because it accumulates evidence over many timesteps rather than looking at single samples.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **BOCD** is slower but can adapt — its predictive model accounts for the observation variance.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Window** needs a large enough shift that the two windows' Bernoulli distributions diverge measurably.\n",
    "\n",
    "**For CSIRO (Sanders / Usman):**  This chart tells you the *detection limit* of the toolkit.  For a given device and number of shots, you can read off which shifts are detectable.  If you are running a quantum verification experiment that lasts 200 timesteps with 500 shots, any drift larger than ~$\\Delta\\mu = 0.02$ will be caught by the ensemble with recall > 0.9."
