    "### Interpretation — Part D\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ensemble advantage:**\n",
    "The ensemble's recall is higher than any single detector across all noise types.  Its false-alarm rate is also the lowest — because a single fluke spike in one detector doesn't trigger the alarm unless a second detector agrees.  The cost is a slightly longer mean delay — combining three detectors, which each have their own latency, pushes the *earliest* alarm slightly later.  But this is a good trade-off: in practice, a false alarm means a wasted manual inspection; a missed detection means corrupted experimental data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Individual detector personalities:**\n",
    "- **CUSUM** has the lowest mean delay (it is the fastest) and high recall on depolarizing noise.  It struggles on correlated noise because the AR(1) process violates its i.i.d. assumption.\n",
    "- **BOCD** is the most principled and has low false alarm rates, but needs several timesteps to accumulate a confident posterior — hence the longer delay.\n",
    "- **Window detector** is reliable across noise types but needs a sufficient window length, which naturally introduces a delay of at least one window width."
